    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "Sets as integers",
      "question": "How do you store a set in an integer, and loop over all its subsets?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Bitmask D P, question one. How do you store a set in an integer, and how do you loop over all of its subsets? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "A set in an <em>integer</em>: how, and how to loop its subsets?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "With n items, n at most about twenty, bit i says whether item i is in the set. Adding item i is an or with one shifted left by i. Testing it is a shift right, and an and with one. Removing it ands with the complement. And popcount gives the size. The full set is one shifted left by n, minus one.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Bit i = <em>item i</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "Adding",
                "label": "Add",
                "title": "Set bit i",
                "body": "",
                "code": "mask | (1 << i)"
              },
              {
                "at": "Testing",
                "label": "Test",
                "title": "Is i in the set?",
                "body": "",
                "code": "(mask >> i) & 1"
              },
              {
                "at": "Removing",
                "label": "Remove",
                "title": "Clear bit i",
                "body": "",
                "code": "mask & ~(1 << i)"
              },
              {
                "at": "popcount",
                "label": "Size",
                "title": "Count the bits",
                "body": "Full set: (1 << n) − 1.",
                "code": "__builtin_popcount(mask)"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "To visit every subset of a mask, start at the mask itself, and repeat: subtract one, then and with the mask. Subtracting one clears the lowest set bit, and sets every bit below it. The and throws away the bits outside the mask. For one zero one one, that walks through exactly seven subsets, in decreasing order, and never touches a value outside the mask. The loop stops at zero, so handle the empty set separately if you need it.",
