        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "d p of mask and v is the cheapest path that visits exactly the nodes in mask, and ends at v. Every single node is a free start. Masks only grow, so looping masks in increasing order computes each state before it's read. From each state, extend to any unvisited node. The answer is the best over the full mask. That's two to the n times n states, each with n moves: O of two to the n, times n squared. Fine up to about twenty nodes, where n factorial is hopeless.",
          "props": {
            "title": "dp[mask][v]",
            "file": "visit_all.cpp",
            "tag": "C++",
            "lang": "cpp",
            "size": 19,
            "width": 1300,
            "code": "int shortestPath(int n, const vector<vector<int>>& cost) {\n    const int INF = 1e9, full = (1 << n) - 1;\n    // dp[mask][v]: cheapest path visiting exactly mask, ending at v\n    vector<vector<int>> dp(1 << n, vector<int>(n, INF));\n    for (int v = 0; v < n; ++v) dp[1 << v][v] = 0;    // start anywhere\n    for (int mask = 1; mask <= full; ++mask)\n        for (int v = 0; v < n; ++v) {\n            if (!(mask >> v & 1) || dp[mask][v] == INF) continue;\n            for (int u = 0; u < n; ++u) {\n                if (mask >> u & 1) continue;              // already visited\n                int next = mask | 1 << u;\n                dp[next][u] = min(dp[next][u], dp[mask][v] + cost[v][u]);\n            }\n        }\n    return *min_element(dp[full].begin(), dp[full].end());\n}",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "exactly",
                "lines": "3",
                "note": "State: (visited set, last node)"
